Load balancer evenly distributes traffic to different web servers


As traffic increases:
- A single server becomes overloaded.
- Response time increases.
- System may crash.


server = hash(requestID) % N
Where:
- N = number of servers

Example:
If N = 4:
server = hash(requestID) % 4

This distributes requests across servers roughly evenly.

Now you add one more server:
N = 5 servers
server = hash(requestID) % 5


Problem:
- The modulo value changes.
- Almost all request-to-server mappings change.
- Requests that earlier went to Server 2 may now go to Server 4.
- Cached data on old servers becomes useless.
- System experiences heavy cache invalidation.
- Performance drops significantly.


Basic hashing works for distributing load.

But when servers are added or removed:
- Standard modulo-based hashing breaks.
- It causes large-scale redistribution.
- It invalidates cache.

This causes a full reshuffling of users across servers.
